  "r_exemplars_note": "the DEFLATED count -- a stem replicated across studies inflates the raw figure, severely for ar (395 -> 89). Never quote the undeflated number.",
  "batch_note": "batches 0-2 are decided; 3 and later are PROVISIONAL and carry the family's provisional position.",
  "sas_breadth_jobs": "2026-09-02-job-census-summary.json. Distinct studies with a PROGRAM file of this prefix: program extension, folder other than estimates, not a template, and placed. sas_breadth is retained unchanged and still counts every extension, so the two are comparable rather than one replacing the other. See dev/specs/2026-09-02-per-folder-naming-parse-design.md. Both are DISTINCT-STUDY counts and must not be summed across rows.",
  "qualifier": "Job type within the prefix, matching the template filename's optional third field. null marks an UNQUALIFIED row, which is every row today. A prefix may hold several rows, one per qualifier, because graphs/dp is trends, spaghetti, procs and more under one prefix; it must be wholly qualified or wholly unqualified, so a null row is the only row for its prefix. Rows are keyed on (prefix, qualifier). See dev/specs/2026-09-02-dp-dc-decomposition-design.md, section 8.",
  "decomposed_2026_09_03": {
   "what": "The single `dp` and `dc` rows were replaced by one row per job type, enacting section 9 of dev/specs/2026-09-02-dp-dc-decomposition-design.md.",
   "why_no_ordinals": "An ordinal is assigned when the template is CREATED and a retired one is never reissued, so assigning twelve now to templates that may never be written would burn them. Every row here is queued with ordinal null, as `dp` and `dc` were.",
   "prefix_totals_before": {
    "dc": {
     "sas_breadth": 1010,
     "sas_breadth_jobs": 1009,
     "r_exemplars": 69,
     "r_jobs": 108
    },
    "dp": {
     "sas_breadth": 628,
     "sas_breadth_jobs": 515,
     "r_exemplars": 323,
     "r_jobs": 1780
    }
   },
   "note": "Prefix-level totals are kept here because the split rows cannot carry them: a study running both dp.trends and dp.gfup is one study in dp and appears in two rows, so the rows must not be summed."
  }
 },
